1
00:00:03,214 --> 00:00:07,620
Privacy requests and breaches have legal deadlines, and most teams find out

2
00:00:07,620 --> 00:00:11,204
about them from a shared inbox when they have already passed.

3
00:00:11,654 --> 00:00:15,548
Here every open request and breach is a clock on one tower: the

4
00:00:15,548 --> 00:00:19,874
round dials are requests, the two hexagons in the belfry are breaches.

5
00:00:20,324 --> 00:00:23,766
The sweep is the share of the legal time already

6
00:00:23,766 --> 00:00:27,424
used; overdue dials crack and their hands tick red.

7
00:00:28,607 --> 00:00:31,298
Switch the lens to type and the floors

8
00:00:31,298 --> 00:00:34,697
regroup by access, erasure and the other rights.

9
00:00:35,502 --> 00:00:38,751
Today ranks what needs me; the first item flies to the

10
00:00:38,751 --> 00:00:42,542
open breach, about forty hours from its seventy-two hour limit.

11
00:00:43,357 --> 00:00:47,022
In the Escapement the clock counts to the second, and

12
00:00:47,022 --> 00:00:50,617
closing stays blocked until a decision is on record.

13
00:00:51,067 --> 00:00:53,737
I rate the risk, decide to notify the authority,

14
00:00:53,737 --> 00:00:56,407
and the decision goes straight into the logbook.

15
00:00:57,214 --> 00:01:00,478
View the tower as a system owner and only

16
00:01:00,478 --> 00:01:03,743
the clocks with a task for them stay lit.

17
00:01:06,252 --> 00:01:10,140
Logging a request received on the thirty-first of January shows its

18
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:12,752
due date as I type: the last day of February.

19
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:17,508
An extension adds up to two months, but only if the person was

20
00:01:17,508 --> 00:01:22,030
told within the first month, so for this overdue request it is blocked.

21
00:01:22,831 --> 00:01:28,291
System owners get their search, export and erase tasks on the Gears board.

22
00:01:28,741 --> 00:01:31,901
When Sam marks his CRM erasure done, the gear

23
00:01:31,901 --> 00:01:35,131
turns and the request dial fills in a segment.

24
00:01:35,940 --> 00:01:38,848
Every step lands in a logbook nobody can edit,

25
00:01:38,848 --> 00:01:41,630
in order and ready to print for a regulator.

26
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:45,507
The Almanac shows how the clocks have run: on time by

27
00:01:45,507 --> 00:01:49,710
month, days to close, and every breach against seventy-two hours.

28
00:01:50,514 --> 00:01:52,887
Clocks you can see and steps you can

29
00:01:52,887 --> 00:01:55,854
prove: that is what a privacy deadline needs.
